                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Montonen-Olive+duality"} Montonen-Olive duality
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg+duality"} Seiberg duality
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} quantum geometric Langlands correspondence
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/Langlands+correspondence+--+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#description"} Description
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalizations"} Generalizations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Electric-magnetic duality is a lift of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge duality
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
          %h2#description Description
          %p
            Consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            on a space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "-branes)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            this is the coupling of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            to particles. For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
            this is the coupling of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            to strings.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\nabla \in \Omega^{n+1}(X)
            is a closed
            = succeed "-form." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star F_\nabla
            under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
            is itself closed
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{dR} \star F_\nabla = 0
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            for the standard (abelian
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on the space of circle n-bundle with connection.
          %p
            If this is the case, it makes sense to ask if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star F_\nabla
            itself is the curvature
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d-(n+1))
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d-(n+1)-1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde \nabla
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mi dim
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = dim X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            If such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde \nabla
            exists, its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            is the gauge interaction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo −
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d-n-3)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In the special case of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4
            we have that electrically charged 0-dimensional particles couple to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            and magnetically charged
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 4
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4-(1+1)-2) = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde \nabla
          %p
            In analogy to this case one calls generally the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo −
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d-n-3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde \nabla
            the
            %strong magnetic duals
            of the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d= 4
            EM-duality is the special abelian case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconductors"} superconductors
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superinsulators"} superinsulators
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D2+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=2 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
                electric-magnetic duality is studied as
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+theory"} Seiberg-Witten theory
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
                one considers 1-dimensional objects in
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mn 10
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d=10
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+heterotic+string+theory"} dual heterotic string theory
            %li
              %div
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric-magnetic+duality"} electric-magnetic duality
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric charge
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D0-brane"} D0-brane
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-brane"} D6-brane
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D1-brane"} D1-brane
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D5-brane"} D5-brane
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D2-brane"} D2-brane
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D4-brane"} D4-brane
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-brane"} D3-brane
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-brane"} D3-brane
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_4
              which the M2-brane couples to is the Hodge-dual of the 7-form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_7
              which the M5-brane couples to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 7
                  %mo =
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G_7 = \star G_4
          %p
            as imposed by the equations of motion for 11d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/torsion+constraints+in+supergravity#Examples11dSuGra"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C3-field"} C3-field
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C6-field"} C6-field
            are electric-magnetic duals.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+physics"}> duality in physics
            ,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parent+action+functional"} parent action functional
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %strong electro-magnetic duality
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Montonen-Olive+duality"} Montonen-Olive duality
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+graviton"} dual graviton
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+photon"} dual photon
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg+duality"} Seiberg duality
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} quantum geometric Langlands correspondence
          %h2#references References
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jos%C3%A9+Figueroa-O%27Farrill"}> José Figueroa-O'Farrill
                ,
                %em Electromagnetic Duality for Children
                (1998) (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/FigueroaElectromagneticDuality.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sebastian+De+Haro"}> Sebastian De Haro
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"} Jeremy Butterfield
              %p
                %em> The Philosophy and Physics of Duality
                , Cambridge University Press (2025) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.01616"}> arXiv:2508.01616
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/the-philosophy-and-physics-of-duality-9780198846338"} ISBN:9780198846338
          %p It was originally noticed in
          %ul
            %li#GNO77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Goddard"}> Peter Goddard
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Nuyts"} Jean Nuyts
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Olive"} David Olive
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gauge Theories And Magnetic Charge
              %strong 125
              (1977) 1-28 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(77)90221-8"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(77)90221-8
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/111435"} spire:111435
          %p
            that where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric charge
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            takes values in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+lattice"} weight lattice
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            takes values in the lattice of what is now called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+dual+group"} Langlands dual group
          %p This led to the electric/magnetic duality conjecture formulation in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claus+Montonen"}> Claus Montonen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Olive"} David Olive
              %em Magnetic Monopoles As Gauge Particles?
              Phys. Lett. B72 (1977) 117-120 (
              %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/121372"}> spire:121372
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(77)90076-4"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(77)90076-4
          %p
            According to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KapustinWitten06"} Kapustin &amp; Witten 2006, pages 3-4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+dual+group"} Langlands dual group
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
              , private communication to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              (1977)
          %p
            See also the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
          %p
            On electro-magnetic duality of non-linear electromagnetism (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DBI+theory"}> DBI theory
            ):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gary+Gibbons"}> Gary Gibbons
                , D. A. Rasheed:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Electric-Magnetic Duality Rotations in Non-Linear Electrodynamics
                %strong 454
                1-2 (1995) 185-206 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9506035"}> arXiv:hep-th/9506035
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(95)00409-L"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(95)00409-L
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gary+Gibbons"}> Gary Gibbons
                , D. A. Rasheed:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi SL
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(2,\mathbb{R})
                    Invariance of Non-Linear Electrodynamics Coupled to An Axion and a Dilaton
                %strong 365
                1-4 (1996) 46-50 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9509141"}> arXiv:hep-th/9509141
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(95)01272-9"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(95)01272-9
          %p
            The insight that the Montonen-Olive duality works more naturally in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Olive"}> David Olive
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em Supersymmetry Algebras That Include Topological Charges
          %p
            and that it works particularly for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D4+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=4 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              H. Osborn,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Topological Charges For
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = 4
                  Supersymmetric Gauge Theories And Monopoles Of Spin 1
          %p
            The observation that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            electric/magnetic duality extends to an
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(2,\mathbb{Z})
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Cardy"}> John Cardy
                , E. Rabinovici,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Phase Structure Of Zp Models In The Presence Of A Theta Parameter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Cardy"}> John Cardy
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Duality And The Theta Parameter In Abelian Lattice Models
            %li
              %p
                A. Shapere and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Wilczek"} Frank Wilczek
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Selfdual Models With Theta Terms
          %p
            and specifically the embedding of this into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            originates in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ashoke+Sen"}> Ashoke Sen
              ,
              %em
                Dyon - Monopole Bound States, Self-Dual Harmonic Forms on the Multi-Monopole Moduli Space, and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SL
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(2,\mathbb{Z})
                Invariance in String Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9402032"}> arXiv:hep-th/9402032
              )
          %p
            The understanding of this
            = succeed "-symmetry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(2,\mathbb{Z})
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            – compactified on a torus is described in
          %ul
            %li#Witten95a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                , pages 4-5 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some Comments On String Dynamics
                %em String95
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hepth/9507121"}> arXiv:hepth/9507121
                )
            %li#Witten95b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                %em On S-Duality in Abelian Gauge Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9505186"}> arXiv:hep-th/9505186
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cumrun+Vafa"}> Cumrun Vafa
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric Origin of Montonen-Olive Duality
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9707131"} arXiv:hep-th/9707131
            %li#Witten07
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Conformal+field+theory+in+four+and+six+dimensions"} Conformal field theory in four and six dimensions
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrike+Tillmann"} Ulrike Tillmann
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topology, Geometry and Quantum Field Theory: Proceedings of the 2002 Oxford Symposium in Honour of the 60th Birthday of Graeme Segal
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0157"} arXiv:0712.0157
          %p
            The relation of S-duality to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+duality"} geometric Langlands duality
            was understood in
          %ul
            %li#KapustinWitten06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anton+Kapustin"}> Anton Kapustin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              %em Electric-Magnetic Duality And The Geometric Langlands Program
              , Communications in number theory and physics, Volume 1, Number 1, 1–236 (2007) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0604151"}> arXiv:hep-th/0604151
              )
          %p Exposition of this is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Frenkel"}> Edward Frenkel
              ,
              %em What Do Fermat’s Last Theorem and Electro-magnetic Duality Have in Common?
              KITP talk 2011 (
              %a{:href => "http://online.kitp.ucsb.edu/online/bblunch/frenkel/"}> web
              )
          %p
            A perspective unifying EM-duality of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theories"} higher gauge theories
            with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+T-duality"} non-abelian T-duality
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%A1n+Pulmann"}> Ján Pulmann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavol+%C5%A0evera"} Pavol Ševera
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fridrich+Valach"} Fridrich Valach
              %em A non-abelian duality for (higher) gauge theories
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06151"}> arXiv:1909.06151
              )
          %p An arithmetic version of EM-duality:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"}> David Ben-Zvi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yiannis+Sakellaridis"} Yiannis Sakellaridis
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Akshay+Venkatesh"} Akshay Venkatesh
              %em Relative Langlands Duality
              (
              %a{:href => "https://www.math.ias.edu/~akshay/research/BZSVpaperV1.pdf"}> pdf
              )
        .revisedby
